<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  This file exists for the keys `INFOPLIST_KEY_*` cannot carry. `GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE`
	  is still YES — the scene manifest and the orientations continue to arrive as
	  `INFOPLIST_KEY_*` build settings and are merged over this base, the same
	  arrangement the macOS app (`../Info.plist`) and `AtelierRefsShare` already use.
	  The App Group key is here rather than a seventh `INFOPLIST_KEY_*` because that
	  mechanism covers only the Info.plist keys Xcode knows:
	  `INFOPLIST_KEY_AtelierAppGroupIdentifier` was accepted by the build without
	  complaint and dropped from the generated plist, which is a silent failure the app
	  would have reported at runtime as `appGroupIdentifierMissing` (092 · S4b).

	  092 · S1 (decision 2) — the App Group whose container holds the Library, read by
	  `LibraryLocation.appGroupIdentifier()`. Fed by the same $(ATELIER_APP_GROUP)
	  build setting as `AtelierRefsMobile.entitlements`, so the identifier this
	  process ASKS for and the identifier it is GRANTED cannot disagree.
	-->
	<key>AtelierAppGroupIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(ATELIER_APP_GROUP)</string>
	<!--
	  093 § 6 — dark only. The Mac commits twice (`.preferredColorScheme(.dark)` AND
	  `NSApp.appearance`), and the phone does the same: `.preferredColorScheme(.dark)`
	  on the root view, and this key so the SYSTEM chrome the app does not draw —
	  the status bar, the keyboard, a share sheet presented over it — matches. The
	  share extension already carries its own copy (`AtelierRefsShare/Info.plist`),
	  since an extension is its own bundle and does not inherit the host's preference.
	  A light system chrome around a dark app is the worst of both.
	-->
	<key>UIUserInterfaceStyle</key>
	<string>Dark</string>
	<!--
	  093 § 6 again, one frame earlier. `INFOPLIST_KEY_UILaunchScreen_Generation = YES`
	  generated an EMPTY `UILaunchScreen` dictionary, which means `systemBackground` —
	  and `systemBackground` under `UIUserInterfaceStyle = Dark` is pure `#000000`. So
	  every cold launch drew one black frame before `canvasOuter` `#131313` painted:
	  a flash, on the ground tone the whole app is committed to, in the first thing
	  anybody sees. A launch screen is not a screen here, it is the colour the app
	  starts as, so it names the colour.

	  `UIColorName` rather than a `UIImageName` or a storyboard: there is nothing to
	  draw. The value is an asset-catalog colour set (`Assets.xcassets/LaunchBackground`)
	  because that is the only form this key takes — UIKit resolves it through
	  `UIColor(named:)` in the LAUNCH process, which has no Swift of ours running in it
	  and therefore cannot read `Tokens.Colors.canvasOuter`. That makes the colour set a
	  second spelling of `0x131313` (`AtelierTokens/Palette.swift:26`), which is why it
	  is written there in hex rather than as decimal components: the two are meant to be
	  comparable by eye.

	  `INFOPLIST_KEY_UILaunchScreen_Generation` is REMOVED from both configurations, not
	  set alongside this: the generated keys are merged OVER this file, so leaving it on
	  would put the empty dictionary back and the flash with it. Written here rather than
	  as `INFOPLIST_KEY_UILaunchScreen_UIColorName` so the value sits next to the two
	  paragraphs that say why it is that value — a build setting is a string with its
	  argument removed, which is the same case the App Group key above makes.
	-->
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIColorName</key>
		<string>LaunchBackground</string>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
